{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General bias: model is systematically too low versus realized prices during rising regimes (most days in examples). Apply an upward level shift of roughly +1.0 to +2.5 EUR across much of the horizon, strongest in the first ~20 days.",
    "Horizon shape error: model underestimates mid-horizon strength (≈day 7–20) where realized prices stay elevated; increase forecasts more in this window than at the very front.",
    "Late-horizon reversal risk: model often overpredicts the tail when it extrapolates a rally (e.g., forecasts pushing into 70–72 while realized falls back to mid-60s). Add a mean-reversion cap/discount for days ~25–30, especially if the model trend is strongly up.",
    "Spike handling: after sharp up-moves, the model tends to fade too quickly (underpredicts persistence). Reduce post-spike mean-reversion speed; keep elevated levels for ~1–2 weeks longer than the model implies.",
    "Downturn timing: the model is late to price sudden late-horizon drops (it keeps rising while realized turns down). Introduce a regime-switch trigger: if recent history shows volatility + stretched upside, force a flatter/declining path in the last third of the horizon."
  ]
}
